{"id": "2026-08-01-news-ceuta-crossing-toll-rises-to-67", "date": "2026-08-01", "stream": "news", "headline": "Ceuta crossing toll rises to 67", "summary": "At least 67 died after some 50,000 people crossed from Morocco into Spain's enclave of Ceuta in a day, splitting the EU over Schengen.", "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/31/ceuta-spain-morocco-border-deaths", "source_domain": "theguardian.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "The death toll from the mass crossing into Spain's north-African enclave of Ceuta rose to at least 67 on Saturday 1 August, a day after roughly 50,000 people entered the tiny territory from Morocco in about 24 hours. The influx on 30 July overwhelmed a city of 85,000 and triggered a political crisis in Madrid; Spain's interior ministry said more than 48,300 migrants had voluntarily returned to Morocco by Friday evening, and El Mundo reported the enclave was gradually returning to normal by Saturday. Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez, who visited Ceuta on Friday, attributed the surge to human-trafficking mafias exploiting a recent Spanish supreme-court ruling that bars the summary return of migrants intercepted at sea, and said Madrid would install a line of buoys at Tarajal beach to mark the border in line with that ruling. The episode has split the EU: Italy suspended its Schengen arrangement with Spain, and Sweden and Finland backed calls to suspend Spain from the free-movement zone, while Sanchez wrote to Brussels denouncing a selfish, polarising and illegal reaction and requested an emergency videoconference of the 27 interior ministers. The Irish EU presidency convened member states on Saturday under the Integrated Political Crisis Response mechanism, with a further meeting of EU ambassadors called for Monday 4 August.", "why": "This is the largest single-day breach of an EU external border on record, dwarfing the ~10,000 who crossed at Ceuta in May 2021, and it has turned a Spain-Morocco border incident into an open fight over Schengen solidarity that reaches every member state, Switzerland included as a Schengen associate.", "thread_id": "2026-07-31-news-40-000-migrants-cross-into-ceuta-from-morocco", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-31", "event_date": "2026-07-30", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ceuta", "Spain", "Morocco", "Pedro Sanchez", "Schengen"]}
{"id": "2026-08-01-news-us-iran-gulf-war-grinds-on", "date": "2026-08-01", "stream": "news", "headline": "US-Iran Gulf war grinds on", "summary": "Analysts say dwindling US missile stocks in the stalemated war with Iran are delaying arms deliveries, including to Switzerland.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/international/nahost/krieg-gegen-den-iran-patt-am-golf-gehen-den-usa-die-raketen-aus", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The war between the United States and Iran has settled into a stalemate, with Tehran striking commercial shipping every few days and Washington responding in kind but without a durable escalation, SRF's diplomatic correspondent assessed on Friday 31 July. The central question analysts are now asking is whether US munitions, above all interceptor and cruise missiles, are running short: President Trump insists the US has more than it could ever use, yet he has pressed defence firms to accelerate missile production and Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth asked the Senate for roughly $70bn partly to refill shrunken stocks. The think-tank CSIS (Center for Strategic and International Studies) estimates US Tomahawk cruise missiles have fallen to about 2,000 from 3,000 pre-war, Patriot interceptors (the ground-based air-defence missiles) to under 1,000 from about 2,300, and THAAD interceptors to well below half, enough to keep fighting Iran, CSIS's Tom Karako says, but not comfortably alongside a second war in East Asia. One knock-on effect flagged by CSIS analyst Mark Cancian: the war is delaying long-ordered US arms deliveries to other buyers, Switzerland among them. Separately, Al Jazeera reported Iran struck Kuwait and warned of regional flames of war, while Yemen's Houthis denied a plan to charge ships transiting the Red Sea, and researchers cited by SRF noted more than 1,500 large vessels now sit idle around the blocked Strait of Hormuz.", "why": "If the US is genuinely rationing interceptors, that constrains what it can supply to Ukraine and to European buyers, a direct line from the Gulf to Swiss and allied defence procurement, and a vulnerability Beijing is watching.", "thread_id": "2026-08-01-news-us-iran-gulf-war-grinds-on", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-01", "event_date": "2026-07-31", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Iran", "Strait of Hormuz", "CSIS", "Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-08-01-news-russian-missiles-kill-nine-in-kyiv", "date": "2026-08-01", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russian missiles kill nine in Kyiv", "summary": "A Russian overnight barrage on Kyiv killed at least nine and Zelensky blamed a Patriot interceptor shortage.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-zelenskyy-says-patriot-missile-shortage-costs-lives/a-78198568", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A Russian overnight barrage on Kyiv killed at least nine people and injured 23, including four children, in the early hours of Saturday 1 August, Ukraine's State Emergency Service said. President Volodymyr Zelensky said Russia fired 35 missiles, 27 of them ballistic, plus 185 attack drones, and that only a single ballistic missile was intercepted, which he blamed squarely on a shortage of interceptors for Ukraine's Patriot air-defence systems. The strikes damaged 18 residential buildings, a school, and the Lithuanian embassy; Kyiv mayor Vitaly Klitschko said the first two floors of one apartment block partially collapsed, trapping residents. Every night without them results in more casualties, Zelensky said of the missing interceptor packages.", "why": "Zelensky's complaint lands exactly where the Gulf story bites: Ukraine's air defence depends on the same scarce Patriot interceptors that the US war with Iran is now consuming, making Kyiv's civilian casualties partly a function of a competing conflict thousands of kilometres away.", "thread_id": "2026-08-01-news-russian-missiles-kill-nine-in-kyiv", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-01", "event_date": "2026-08-01", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Kyiv", "Volodymyr Zelensky", "Patriot"]}
{"id": "2026-08-01-news-dr-congo-ebola-outbreak-fastest-ever", "date": "2026-08-01", "stream": "news", "headline": "DR Congo Ebola outbreak fastest ever", "summary": "The DR Congo Ebola outbreak became the fastest-growing in the virus's history, with confirmed cases passing 3,200.", "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/jul/31/ebola-outbreak-in-drc-the-fastest-growing-in-the-history-of-the-virus", "source_domain": "theguardian.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["health", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Ebola outbreak in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo has become the fastest-growing in the virus's history, with confirmed cases passing 3,200, The Guardian reported on Friday 31 July. The outbreak, running since June and previously reported near 1,000 deaths, is now spreading faster than any prior Ebola epidemic, and UN agencies say hunger and underfunding are hampering the response.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-06-22-cyber-papers-ebola-cases-surpass-1-000-in-dr-congo-amid-viole", "first_seen_date": "2026-06-22", "event_date": "2026-07-31", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["DR Congo", "Ebola", "WHO"]}
{"id": "2026-08-01-news-bern-apologizes-over-clergy-draft", "date": "2026-08-01", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern apologizes over clergy draft", "summary": "Defence Minister Pfister apologised to the churches for ending clergy's army-service exemption without consulting them.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/dialog/dienstpflicht-fuer-geistliche-bundesrat-entschuldigt-sich-bei-kirchen-fuer-verfahrensfehler", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Defence Minister Martin Pfister has apologised in writing to Switzerland's churches for stripping clergy of their exemption from military service without consulting them first, according to a copy of the letter obtained by RTS and reported Friday 31 July. Since 1 June, priests and pastors have no longer been exempt under a change to the Military Act that the Federal Council introduced without prior announcement or parliamentary debate, so quietly that it went unnoticed at first; nine clergy have since been called up to serve their remaining days. The Defence group made a mistake by not consulting you on the revision of the Military Act. It clearly should have. We ask your forgiveness, Pfister wrote, while confirming the government intends to keep the reform. The Council had justified dropping the exemption by arguing that pastoral care no longer holds the same significance for civil society; Pfister's letter now softens that, acknowledging clergy's value in crisis situations, and Lugano diocese administrator Alain de Raemy welcomed the apology.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-08-01-news-bern-apologizes-over-clergy-draft", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-01", "event_date": "2026-07-31", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Martin Pfister", "Federal Council"]}
{"id": "2026-08-01-news-swiss-army-curbs-chinese-connected-cars", "date": "2026-08-01", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss army curbs Chinese connected cars", "summary": "The Swiss defence department barred internet-connected Chinese vehicles near sensitive military sites, citing espionage risk.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/dialog/vernetzte-autos-aus-china-armee-ergreift-schutzmassnahmen-gegen-smartphones-auf-raedern", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Swiss defence department (VBS) has barred permanently internet-connected vehicles, Chinese models in particular, from the vicinity of especially sensitive military sites, RTS reported on Friday 31 July. One such site is the electronic-warfare centre at Zimmerwald near Bern; the VBS declined to detail the access restrictions, saying they depend on each location's security setup. The move follows an unusually blunt warning from the Federal Intelligence Service (NDB), whose director Serge Bavaud told a late-June press conference that connected cars are smartphones on wheels whose manufacturers cooperate with Chinese authorities, giving intelligence services potential access to recorded data including conversations. Carmaker BYD says it transmits no data to China and strictly respects all European and Swiss data-protection rules; other Chinese makers did not respond to RTS. Switzerland is not alone: German intelligence has run tests on several Chinese brands and the state of Bavaria has opened an inquiry.", "why": "It is a concrete instance of geopolitical mistrust reaching into everyday consumer technology on Swiss soil, and a signal to buyers of increasingly popular Chinese EVs that the state now treats their onboard sensors as a surveillance surface.", "thread_id": "2026-08-01-news-swiss-army-curbs-chinese-connected-cars", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-01", "event_date": "2026-07-31", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "China", "BYD", "NDB"]}
